{"id":"906dbbde-5d44-4305-86b5-74f947de3975","object":"chat.completion","created":1793847367,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"\n\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ calls>\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ invoke name=\"read_range\">\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter name=\"path\" string=\"true\">deepinfer/environ.py</｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter>\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter name=\"start_line\" string=\"false\">1</｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter>\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter name=\"end_line\" string=\"false\">30</｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter>\n</｜｜DSML｜｜ invoke>\n</｜｜DSML｜｜ calls>","reasoning_content":"I have all the mechanisms exposed. Now let me write the modifications.\n\n1. environ.py: add a factory method `int_or_none_min` or just a custom lambda check. Requirements: unset -> None; set -> int parse, must be >= 32; empty string, unparseable, or <32 raise ValueError containing variable name. The descriptor wraps exceptions with f\"{self.name}: {e}\" so the variable name appears. But careful: for int parse failure, `int(\"\")` raises ValueError \"invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''\" which gets wrapped as \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: ...\". Good.\n\nAdd:\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int = 32) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\nHmm, but `int(\"\")` — v is \"\" not None, so int(\"\") raises ValueError. Good.\n\nThen in envs Logging section:\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)\n```\n\nWait — the descriptor's __set_name__ registers in _descriptors, so rearm_all covers it.\n\nNote: descriptor `__get__` catches exceptions and raises `type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")`. Good.\n\n2. logger.py: NewLineFormatter add keyword-only param max_message_chars. Validate: None or int >=32, not bool.\n\nFormat method: currently `msg = super().format(record)`. Need to apply truncation to record.message after args substitution, before prefix... Actually the required behavior: budget only limits message body, not prefix, exc_info or stack_info. Then multi-line prefix alignment uses record.message.\n\nApproach: in format, don't mutate the record. Compute:\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        message = record.getMessage()  # after %s substitution\n        truncated = ... \n```\nBut super().format(record) uses record.message set by Formatter.format. We can't just modify record.msg. Instead we can temporarily patch? Better: build the output ourselves? Simpler approach: temporarily set record.message? Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage() itself. Hmm.\n\nOption: use a shallow copy of the record? Requirement says must not rewrite shared record's msg/args/exc_info/stack_info; same record later given to full handler must retain full message. Using a copy is safe. But then `record.message` on the original wouldn't be set — that's fine.\n\nHmm, but does the test check that original record attributes unchanged? A copy approach keeps the original untouched entirely. That's safest.\n\nBut careful: base Formatter.format with a copied record — copying via `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)` or `copy.copy(record)`. copy module not in allowed imports list for action code, but that's about the module imports I use in the tool? Actually \"Imports allowed\" applies to my action code, not to the source files I edit. Source files can import copy/warnings. But safer to avoid new imports: I can implement format myself partly.\n\nAlternative cleaner: override format to compute message, truncate, and set it into a simple temporary copy created with `logging.LogRecord`? Hmm.\n\nSimplest robust approach:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = self._truncate_message(message)\n        if truncated is message or truncated == message? \n```\nHmm we need super().format(record) to use the truncated message. Formatter.format does:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        record.message = record.getMessage()\n        if self.usesTime():\n            record.asctime = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\n        s = self.formatMessage(record)\n        ...\n```\nSo it computes record.message itself. Overriding requires either patching record.msg temporarily then restoring.\n\nSafe approach: temporarily save record.msg and record.args, set record.msg = truncated, record.args = (), call super().format(record), then restore msg/args and also restore record.message (super sets record.message to truncated). Requirement 5: \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info；同一记录随后交给完整处理器，仍须保留完整消息\". So we must restore msg, args, and also record.message (since Formatter.format leaves record.message set—next handler's Formatter.format will recompute record.message from msg/args anyway, so it will be correct). But `record.message` being truncated could affect the newline prefix logic... Actually to be safe use a copy.\n\nLet me use `copy.copy(record)`? Or `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)` creates a new record with vars(...) — actually makeLogRecord(dict) creates a LogRecord and updates __dict__. That copies exc_info, stack_info too. Then format the copy. The original record untouched, including record.message not set (which is fine; base Formatter in the other handler sets it).\n\nHmm, but one nuance: if test checks that the full handler (another formatter) gets the full message — since the original record's msg/args are untouched, the other handler's format recomputes full message. \n\nHowever, `logging.makeLogRecord` copies `msg` and `args`. Good. Let's use it, no new imports needed (logging already imported).\n\nAlternatively, I can avoid record copy: compute message, truncate, then set on copy. Let's do:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            # Work on a shallow copy so the shared LogRecord (msg/args/message/exc_info/...)\n            # is never mutated for other handlers.\n            record = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\nWait but makeLogRecord copies dict including \"message\"? If original has message set already (from a previous handler), it'd be copied. Fine.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: super().format(record) sets record.message = record.getMessage() on the copy. Then we need to truncate. Truncation must happen after %-substitution => after getMessage. So:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None:\n            record = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)  # hmm always copy?\n```\nHmm, copying always might be wasteful for the fast path but fine. But careful: does copying break anything like `record.exc_text`? Fine.\n\nActually cleaner: do the copy only when truncation applies (i.e. need to check original message length). Compute original message = record.getMessage() first (that does substitution but doesn't mutate record... getMessage does `if self.args: msg = msg % self.args` locally, doesn't mutate). Then decide:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n        if max_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = self._truncate(message, max_chars)\n            if truncated != message:\n                record = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n                record.msg = truncated\n                record.args = ()\n                record.exc_info = ...\n```\nmakeLogRecord already copies exc_info. Wait, but if record.__dict__ has 'message' key from prior handler, setting msg/args won't matter since Formatter.format overwrites record.message. Right, Formatter.format always sets record.message = record.getMessage(). Good.\n\nHmm, one concern: `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)` — makeLogRecord(dict1) does:\n```python\ndef makeLogRecord(dict1=None):\n    rv = _logRecordFactory(None, None, \"\", 0, \"\", (), None, None)\n    if dict1:\n        rv.__dict__.update(dict1)\n    return rv\n```\nSo no class mutations. Fine. But it copies 'args' as the same tuple — we then set to (). Fine.\n\nAlternatively simpler and explicit: temporary save/restore:\n\n```python\n        saved = (record.msg, record.args, record.message if 'message' in record.__dict__ ...)\n```\nCopy is cleaner and safer. I'll use copy via makeLogRecord.\n\nActually, even simpler and fully explicit without relying on makeLogRecord semantics: use a small helper that constructs a copy through `logging.LogRecord`? No, makeLogRecord is fine and standard.\n\nHmm — one risk: the course integration check \"执行从统计源码原样提取的队列消费函数\" — it extracts `_log_worker` and runs it with a real logger. And checks full stats message not truncated and diagnostic truncated. Our implementation should work.\n\nAnother consideration: truncation markers. Budget 32: marker \"...<omitted>...\" is 15 chars. Remaining = 32-15 = 17. Head = ceil(17/2)=9, tail = 17-9=8. Example says \"预算32时，保留开头9字符、末尾8字符，加上15字符标记\". So head = ceil(remaining/2), tail = remaining - head. Wait ceil(17/2)=9, tail=8. ✓. So head = (remaining + 1)//2, tail = remaining - head.\n\nBut hold on: \"剩余空间为'预算减去标记长度'，开头保留向上取整的一半，末尾保留其余部分\". Yes head = ceil(remaining/2).\n\nEdge: if max >= 32, remaining >= 32-15 = 17 > 0 always, so no degenerate case.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\n_TRUNCATION_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n        if len(message) <= max_chars:\n            return message\n        marker = _TRUNCATION_MARKER\n        remaining = max_chars - len(marker)\n        head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n        tail = remaining - head\n        return f\"{message[:head]}{marker}{message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else ''}\"\n```\nCareful: if tail == 0, `message[len(message)-0:]` = whole string — must guard. remaining>=17 so tail>=8 always. But guard anyway.\n\nMulti-line handling: existing code uses `record.message` to compute prefix. After we replace record.message with truncated text... The existing logic:\n\n```python\nmsg = super().format(record)\nif \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n    prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n    msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n```\nThat uses record.message (of the copy, so the truncated version). If truncated message's tail contains newline, still works since record.message is the truncated text used in msg. Fine. But note: with truncated message, if the truncated text contains \"\\n\", alignment uses truncated message as reference in the formatted string — msg.find(truncated) works because msg contains the truncated message. Good.\n\nHmm, one subtlety: what if truncation removes all newlines but the original had newlines? Then msg has no \"\\n\" → no alignment. That's fine.\n\nWait, but requirement 5: prefix alignment preserved for truncated messages presumably fine.\n\nAlso requirement: the budget only limits the message body, not exc_info text. Since we only truncate record.message, exc_info appended afterwards is untouched. ✓\n\nValidation in __init__:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars: int | None = None) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(\n                    f\"max_message_chars must be None or an int >= 32, got {max_message_chars!r}\"\n                )\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nSetup logger: pass `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Need import: `from deepinfer.environ import envs` — check for circular imports? deepinfer/environ.py imports? Let me check environ imports: it imports os, threading, timedelta, etc. Let's check whether environ imports logger. Need to verify. Let me read the head of environ.py.\n\nAlso logger.py currently doesn't import envs. Check deepinfer/__init__.py: `from deepinfer import envs` in stats_logger. environ.py probably has no deepinfer imports → safe.\n\n3. stats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nAlso need to keep run.log and patch.diff? The task description says generate run.log and complete patch.diff containing three files. The course contract verify_commands is pytest. There may be course tooling (eval_deepinfer.py) producing those. I'll run pytest and maybe generate patch.diff myself. Let's check for existing scripts. Progress says changed_files none, and required checks 1.\n\nLet me read environ.py head to confirm imports."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10242,"completion_tokens":3049,"total_tokens":13291,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2972},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":6146},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}